---
Part XIII, Chapter 2: The kinds tropes declare
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The kinds tropes declare

A trope extends Loom's language by declaring kinds. Under contract 1 a kind is a kind of box, of section, of anchor, or of loom. A trope that declares the `Mermaid` kind of box lets every loom of the book write a box `::: Cells {Mermaid}`. Before any trope declares it, such a box is a fault, so a misspelt kind never passes for content. A trope that declares the `Option` kind of section lets every loom write a heading `## Postgres {Option}`, with the parameters the kind takes. A trope that declares the `Tasks` kind of loom lets a book hold a loom whose identity line reads `Tasks 1: The next release`, where a chapter's reads `Chapter 1`. A trope may also let any heading carry a key, whatever kind its section claims.

Loom applies what tropes declare without running any trope. It gathers every trope's declared kinds into the registry, a set of tables it consults for each box, each heading, and each anchor of the book, and it refuses a trope's root whose manifest it cannot read or whose kinds clash with kinds already taken.

A declared kind of section changes how a loom is read, so the registry must exist before any loom is built with it. Loom therefore gathers the registry from the chapters of tropes alone, each trope's root and its members, each read with core Loom. It then builds every loom with what the registry holds, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes. A manifest is read the same whatever its own trope declares.

A manifest is a section's code, so it may hold anchors, and each anchor composes the code of the section it names into the manifest, as an anchor does in any code. Such a section must stand in the manifest's own trope, in the root or in a member. So a trope may keep each of its declarations in the chapter that explains it, and compose them into one manifest. A section in a chapter of another trope, or of no trope, is not the manifest's to compose, since a trope declares what it ships.

Every step is Loom's own code, a lookup and a composition in memory, so the registry fits wherever Loom judges a loom: in the editor on every keystroke, and before every tangle.

## The registry

`DeclaredKinds` is the registry. It holds the `Declarations` of every trope, and `refusals`, every root Loom refuses and why. A `Trope` gathers one trope's chapters: its name, its `chapters`, every chapter that names the trope, and its `root`, when it has one.

`Declarations` holds a table for each sort of kind, `box`, `section`, `anchor`, and `loom`, and `headings`, the keys any heading may carry. A `DeclaredBox` records a kind of box: its name, the format of its body, the `parameters` a box of the kind may carry, and the trope that declared it, with the loom of the trope's root. When the kind's body is data and the kind gives a schema, the record holds its `data` as well, the format with the codec that decodes a body. A `DeclaredSection` and a `DeclaredAnchor` record a kind of section and a kind of anchor the same way, without a body. Each carries the rule its kind follows: a `DeclaredSection` says whether the kind is `codeless`, and a `DeclaredAnchor` says where the kind `standsIn`, when it keeps to one place. A `DeclaredLoom` records a kind of loom with everything its manifest says of it: whether a loom of the kind is `numbered`, the `zone` of the book it sits in, whether the kind is `codeless`, and the kinds of section it `holds`. A `DeclaredKey` records one key any heading may carry: the key, the type of its values, and the same trope and loom. `noDeclarations` holds five empty tables, and `declarationsOf` gives the tables of a registry without its refusals. A `Refusal` records a root's loom, the reason Loom refuses it, and the place in the loom the reason concerns.

=>

export type Trope = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly chapters: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly root: Option.Option<LoomModule>
}

export type DeclaredBox = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly body: string
  readonly data: Option.Option<DataBody>
  readonly parameters: Keys
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type DeclaredSection = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly parameters: Keys
  readonly codeless: boolean
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type DeclaredAnchor = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly parameters: Keys
  readonly standsIn: Option.Option<Standing>
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type DeclaredLoom = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly numbered: boolean
  readonly zone: Zone
  readonly codeless: boolean
  readonly holds: ReadonlyArray<string>
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type DeclaredKey = {
  readonly key: string
  readonly type: ValueType
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type Declarations = {
  readonly box: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredBox>
  readonly section: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredSection>
  readonly anchor: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredAnchor>
  readonly loom: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredLoom>
  readonly headings: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKey>
}

export const noDeclarations: Declarations = {
  box: [],
  section: [],
  anchor: [],
  loom: [],
  headings: [],
}

export type Refusal = { readonly module: Location; readonly reason: string; readonly at: Position }

export type DeclaredKinds = Declarations & { readonly refusals: ReadonlyArray<Refusal> }

export const declarationsOf = (kinds: DeclaredKinds): Declarations => ({
  box: kinds.box,
  section: kinds.section,
  anchor: kinds.anchor,
  loom: kinds.loom,
  headings: kinds.headings,
})

## A trope's root and its manifest

A trope is a cluster of chapters, and each of them names the trope in its frontmatter. The chapter that roots the trope is a trope chapter, and its title is the trope's name; `rootsTrope` tells such a chapter. Every other chapter of the trope is a member, and names the trope with a `Rooted in:` line. `tropeOf` reads the trope a chapter roots or belongs to, and `tropeLineOf` finds where it says so: the word `Trope` of a trope chapter, or the value of a `Rooted in:` line. A chapter says all of this in its frontmatter, which the parser has read before any kind is declared. So `tropeIn` reads the trope from a parsed tree alone, for the compiler, which must know the chapters of tropes before it can finish building any loom. `tropesOf` gathers each trope's chapters in the order of their paths, so that when two chapters clash, the same one stands on every machine. A trope whose chapters are all members has no root yet.

The root is also where the trope's manifest belongs, a section of the `Manifest` kind, which `manifestSectionsOf` finds. A manifest anywhere else is the product pass's to fault, as the chapter "The product pass" describes. A root with two manifests leaves Loom unsure which to read, so Loom refuses it. `manifestOf` composes the one manifest into its text with the product pass's own composition, `fromCode`, so an anchor in the manifest draws in the code it names. The composed text then goes to `readManifest`, as the chapter "The trope contract" describes. Each refusal of a manifest stands at the manifest's heading.

A manifest is composed against the chapters of its own trope alone, so an anchor in it that names a section of any other chapter finds nothing there. A composition leaves such an anchor's place empty, and the manifest would read as if the author had declared less. `composedOf` therefore asks `unfoundIn` for the names the manifest's anchors cannot find, and refuses the manifest for the first of them.

=>

const tropeLine = (doc: ParsedDocument): Option.Option<FrontmatterTropeToken> =>
  Option.orElse(Option.fromNullishOr(doc.frontmatter?.trope), () =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(doc.frontmatter?.rootedIn),
  )

export const tropeIn = (doc: ParsedDocument): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(tropeLine(doc), (line) => line.value)

export const tropeOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<string> => tropeIn(module.doc)

export const tropeLineOf = (module: LoomModule): Position =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(tropeLine(module.doc), (line) => line.position),
    () => module.doc.position,
  )

export const rootsTrope = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  module.doc.frontmatter?.trope !== undefined

export const tropesOf = (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>): ReadonlyArray<Trope> => {
  const named = Array.sort(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()).filter((module) => Option.isSome(tropeOf(module))),
    Order.mapInput(Order.String, (module: LoomModule) => module.path),
  )
  return Array.dedupe(Array.getSomes(named.map(tropeOf))).map((name): Trope => {
    const chapters = named.filter((module) => Option.contains(tropeOf(module), name))
    return { name, chapters, root: Array.findFirst(chapters, rootsTrope) }
  })
}

const manifestSectionsOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<LoomSection> =>
  module.doc.sections.filter(isManifest)

type Read = { readonly manifest: TropeManifest; readonly at: Position }

const composedOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  code: Code,
): Result.Result<string, string> =>
  Array.match(unfoundIn(modules, code, catalogue), {
    onEmpty: () => Result.succeed(fromCode(modules, code, catalogue).code),
    onNonEmpty: ([name]) =>
      Result.fail(
        `an anchor in its manifest names \`${name}\`, and no chapter of the trope holds a section called that`,
      ),
  })

const manifestOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifests: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<LoomSection>,
): Result.Result<Read, Refusal> => {
  const [section, ...others] = manifests
  const refusal = (reason: string, at: Position): Refusal => ({ module: root.path, reason, at })
  return Array.match(others, {
    onNonEmpty: ([second]) =>
      Result.fail(refusal('it holds more than one manifest', second.heading.position)),
    onEmpty: () =>
      pipe(
        Array.findFirst(root.product.code, (code) => code.origin.name === sectionNameOf(section)),
        Option.match({
          onNone: (): Result.Result<string, string> => Result.succeed(''),
          onSome: (code) => composedOf(modules, catalogue, code),
        }),
        Result.flatMap(readManifest),
        Result.map((manifest): Read => ({ manifest, at: section.heading.position })),
        Result.mapError((reason) => refusal(reason, section.heading.position)),
      ),
  })
}

## Refusing a clash

A kind means one thing in a book, so two tropes cannot both decide what one kind means. `clashOf` takes the kinds a manifest declares for one table, and looks for the first that is already taken. The core keeps its own kinds for itself, so a trope that declares the `Note` kind of box is refused, and so is one that declares the `Tangle` kind of section. A kind an earlier trope declared is taken too, and the root that comes later in the order of paths is refused, with the earlier trope named. A root that declares one kind twice is refused as well. Kinds compare in any case of letters, as braces read them.

Each table is judged alone. A box, a section, and an anchor stand in different places, so a kind of box and a kind of section may share a name.

A key means one thing wherever it stands. `ownKeyOf` looks among the parameters a manifest declares for one bearer for a key the core keeps for that bearer: `lang` or `kind` on a heading, `kind`, `provides`, or `anchors` on a box, and `kind` or `provides` on an anchor. Such a root is refused.

A key any heading may carry must be new to every heading. `headingClash` refuses a root whose key for headings another trope declared for headings already, or which a kind of section lists among its parameters, since a heading of that kind would then hold one key with two meanings. It refuses a root the same way when one of its kinds of section lists a key that another trope lets any heading carry.

A kind of loom is named in an identity line, so its words must not read as a line the frontmatter already has. `Chapter` and `Discussion` are the core's own kinds of loom, `loomKinds`, and a trope that declares either is refused as for any kind of the core's. `lineClash` refuses a kind that holds one of the words of Loom's identity lines, as `Bonus Chapter` does, and a kind that is one of the frontmatter's own keys, such as `Package`.

A kind of loom holds kinds of section, and a trope declares what it ships. `unheldIn` refuses a root whose kind of loom holds a kind of section that the same manifest does not declare.

`refusalIn` gives the first reason a manifest is refused: a clash among its kinds of box, of section, of anchor, or of loom, then a kind of loom the frontmatter cannot name, then a kind of section a loom holds and nobody declares, then a key of the core's, then a clash over a key for headings. `clashReason` words the clash of one kind, by what was found of it.

=>

const sameKind = (one: string, other: string): boolean => one.toLowerCase() === other.toLowerCase()

type Taken = { readonly kind: string; readonly trope: string }

const clashReason = Match.fn(
  (
    _within: {
      readonly kind: string
    },
    found: {
      readonly core: boolean
      readonly earlier: Option.Option<Taken>
      readonly twice: boolean
    },
  ) => found,
).pipe(
  Match.withReturnType<Option.Option<string>>(),
  Match.when({ core: true }, (_found, { kind }) =>
    Option.some(`\`${kind}\` is one of Loom's own kinds`),
  ),
  Match.when({ earlier: Option.isSome }, ({ earlier }, { kind }) =>
    Option.map(earlier, (other) => `the \`${other.trope}\` trope declares \`${kind}\` already`),
  ),
  Match.when({ twice: true }, (_found, { kind }) => Option.some(`it declares \`${kind}\` twice`)),
  Match.orElse(() => Option.none<string>()),
)

const clashOf = (
  declared: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  core: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  taken: ReadonlyArray<Taken>,
): Option.Option<string> =>
  Array.findFirst(declared, (kind, at) =>
    clashReason(
      { kind },
      {
        core: core.some((own) => sameKind(own, kind)),
        earlier: Array.findFirst(taken, (other) => sameKind(other.kind, kind)),
        twice: Array.take(declared, at).some((before) => sameKind(before, kind)),
      },
    ),
  )

const ownKeyOf = (declared: ReadonlyArray<Keys>, own: Keys): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    declared.flatMap((parameters) => Object.keys(parameters)),
    Array.findFirst((key) => Option.isSome(keyType(own, key))),
    Option.map((key) => `\`${key}\` is one of Loom's own keys`),
  )

const kindsIn = (declared: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly kind: string }>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  declared.map((declaration) => declaration.kind)

type KindWithKeys = { readonly kind: string; readonly parameters: Keys }

const headingClash = (manifest: TropeManifest, taken: Declarations): Option.Option<string> => {
  const forHeadings = Object.keys(headingKeysOf(manifest))
  const ownSections = sectionKindsOf(manifest).map((declaration): KindWithKeys => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    parameters: parametersOf(declaration),
  }))
  const sections: ReadonlyArray<KindWithKeys> = [...taken.section, ...ownSections]
  const onHeadings = [
    ...forHeadings,
    ...ownSections.flatMap((section) => Object.keys(section.parameters)),
  ]
  return pipe(
    Array.findFirst(onHeadings, (key) =>
      Array.findFirst(taken.headings, (other) => other.key === key),
    ),
    Option.map(
      (other) => `the \`${other.trope}\` trope lets any heading carry \`${other.key}\` already`,
    ),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Array.findFirst(forHeadings, (key) =>
        Option.map(
          Array.findFirst(sections, (section) => Option.isSome(keyType(section.parameters, key))),
          (section) => `\`${key}\` is a parameter of the \`${section.kind}\` kind of section already`,
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

const lineClash = (manifest: TropeManifest): Option.Option<string> =>
  Array.findFirst(kindsIn(loomKindsOf(manifest)), (kind) =>
    pipe(
      Array.findFirst(kind.split(' '), (word) => identityWords.some((own) => sameKind(own, word))),
      Option.map((word) => `\`${kind}\` holds \`${word}\`, a word of Loom's own identity lines`),
      Option.orElse(() =>
        frontmatterKeys.some((key) => sameKind(key, kind))
          ? Option.some(`\`${kind}\` is a line of the frontmatter already`)
          : Option.none(),
      ),
    ),
  )

const unheldIn = (manifest: TropeManifest): Option.Option<string> =>
  Array.findFirst(loomKindsOf(manifest), (loom) =>
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(
        heldBy(loom),
        (held) => !sectionKindsOf(manifest).some((section) => sameKind(section.kind, held)),
      ),
      (held) =>
        `its \`${loom.kind}\` kind of loom holds \`${held}\`, and its manifest declares no kind of section called that`,
    ),
  )

const refusalIn = (manifest: TropeManifest, taken: Declarations): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    clashOf(kindsIn(boxKindsOf(manifest)), boxKinds, taken.box),
    Option.orElse(() => clashOf(kindsIn(sectionKindsOf(manifest)), sectionKinds, taken.section)),
    Option.orElse(() => clashOf(kindsIn(anchorKindsOf(manifest)), anchorKinds, taken.anchor)),
    Option.orElse(() => clashOf(kindsIn(loomKindsOf(manifest)), loomKinds, taken.loom)),
    Option.orElse(() => lineClash(manifest)),
    Option.orElse(() => unheldIn(manifest)),
    Option.orElse(() => ownKeyOf(boxKindsOf(manifest).map(parametersOf), boxKeys)),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      ownKeyOf(
        [...sectionKindsOf(manifest).map(parametersOf), headingKeysOf(manifest)],
        headingKeys,
      ),
    ),
    Option.orElse(() => ownKeyOf(anchorKindsOf(manifest).map(parametersOf), anchorKeys)),
    Option.orElse(() => headingClash(manifest, taken)),
  )

## Gathering the registry

`declaredKindsOf` builds the registry from a corpus. It gathers the tropes and folds over them, carrying the registry so far, and `withTrope` adds one trope. A workspace may also install tropes from its store, as the chapter "Tropes a workspace installs" describes, and their declarations arrive as `installed`. The fold starts from them, so a trope of the book that declares a kind an installed trope declares is refused, as any later clash is. One trope keeps one root, so a second trope chapter of the same title is refused, at the word `Trope` of its identity line. `titleOf` reads a chapter's title.

A trope declares what it ships, so its manifest is read against its own chapters and no others. `chaptersOf` gathers a trope's chapters, and `withTrope` hands `withRoot` those chapters with a catalogue of them alone.

`withRoot` reads the root's manifest, when the root holds one. A root whose manifest reads and clashes with nothing adds everything it declares. `boxesOf`, `sectionsOf`, `anchorsOf`, and `loomsOf` turn its kinds into rows of the registry, and `headingsOf` does the same for its keys for headings. A kind of loom that leaves `zone` out sits in the body of its book, and one that leaves `numbered` out takes no number. A root Loom refuses adds its refusal and none of its kinds, since the author agreed to the trope as a whole, and a trope that runs half its offer is not the trope the author agreed to.

=>

export const titleOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title), (title) => title.value),
    () => module.path,
  )

const boxesOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredBox> =>
  boxKindsOf(manifest).map((declaration): DeclaredBox => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    body: declaration.body,
    data: dataOf(declaration),
    parameters: parametersOf(declaration),
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const sectionsOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredSection> =>
  sectionKindsOf(manifest).map((declaration): DeclaredSection => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    parameters: parametersOf(declaration),
    codeless: declaration.code === 'none',
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const anchorsOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredAnchor> =>
  anchorKindsOf(manifest).map((declaration): DeclaredAnchor => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    parameters: parametersOf(declaration),
    standsIn: Option.fromNullishOr(declaration.standsIn),
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const loomsOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredLoom> =>
  loomKindsOf(manifest).map((declaration): DeclaredLoom => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    numbered: declaration.numbered === true,
    zone: Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(declaration.zone), (): Zone => 'body'),
    codeless: declaration.code === 'none',
    holds: heldBy(declaration),
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const headingsOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKey> =>
  Object.entries(headingKeysOf(manifest)).map(([key, type]): DeclaredKey => ({
    key,
    type,
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const refused = (kinds: DeclaredKinds, refusal: Refusal): DeclaredKinds => ({
  ...kinds,
  refusals: [...kinds.refusals, refusal],
})

const withRoot = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  kinds: DeclaredKinds,
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
): DeclaredKinds =>
  Array.match(manifestSectionsOf(root), {
    onEmpty: () => kinds,
    onNonEmpty: (manifests) =>
      Result.match(manifestOf(modules, catalogue, root, manifests), {
        onFailure: (refusal) => refused(kinds, refusal),
        onSuccess: ({ manifest, at }) =>
          Option.match(refusalIn(manifest, kinds), {
            onSome: (reason) => refused(kinds, { module: root.path, reason, at }),
            onNone: () => ({
              ...kinds,
              box: [...kinds.box, ...boxesOf(trope, root, manifest)],
              section: [...kinds.section, ...sectionsOf(trope, root, manifest)],
              anchor: [...kinds.anchor, ...anchorsOf(trope, root, manifest)],
              loom: [...kinds.loom, ...loomsOf(trope, root, manifest)],
              headings: [...kinds.headings, ...headingsOf(trope, root, manifest)],
            }),
          }),
      }),
  })

const chaptersOf = (trope: Trope): ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule> =>
  new Map(trope.chapters.map((chapter) => [chapter.path, chapter] as const))

const withTrope = (kinds: DeclaredKinds, trope: Trope): DeclaredKinds => {
  const others = trope.chapters.filter(
    (chapter) => !Option.exists(trope.root, (root) => root.path === chapter.path),
  )
  const duplicates = Option.match(trope.root, {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Refusal> => [],
    onSome: (root) =>
      others.filter(rootsTrope).map((chapter): Refusal => ({
        module: chapter.path,
        reason: `another trope chapter is called \`${titleOf(root)}\``,
        at: tropeLineOf(chapter),
      })),
  })
  const settled: DeclaredKinds = { ...kinds, refusals: [...kinds.refusals, ...duplicates] }
  return Option.match(trope.root, {
    onNone: () => settled,
    onSome: (root) => {
      const own = chaptersOf(trope)
      return withRoot(own, catalogueOf(own), settled, trope.name, root)
    },
  })
}

export const declaredKindsOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  installed: Declarations = noDeclarations,
): DeclaredKinds => {
  const fromInstalled: DeclaredKinds = { ...installed, refusals: [] }
  return tropesOf(modules).reduce(withTrope, fromInstalled)
}

## Judging a loom against the registry

`kindFaults` gives a loom the faults the registry finds in it. A root that Loom refused carries a `TropeRefused` fault with the reason, where the refusal stands.

Every box of the loom that names a kind is judged against the core's kinds and the declared ones. `writtenKind` reads the kind a box's braces name, and where: a label's words, or the value of a `kind` parameter. A label that the tokeniser has already faulted, such as an empty one, is judged no further. A kind that neither the core nor a trope gives a box carries an `UnknownKind` fault, which lists every kind the box could take, the core's first and the declared ones after them.

A box's keys depend on its kind as well. Only a trope box takes `provides` and `anchors`, so `keysOutsideTrope` gives either key on a box of any other kind a `KeyOutsideTrope` fault, on the key itself.

A box of a kind that gives a schema is judged by its body too, and `bodyFaults` gives those faults, as the section "A body that fits its schema" describes.

=>

const writtenKind: (
  specifier: SpecifierToken | ParametersToken,
) => Option.Option<{ readonly kind: string; readonly at: Position }> =
  byType<SpecifierToken | ParametersToken>()({
    Specifier: (labelled) =>
      labelled.label.health.status === 'ok'
        ? Option.some({ kind: labelled.label.value, at: labelled.label.position })
        : Option.none(),
    Parameters: (held) =>
      pipe(
        Array.findFirst(held.parameters, (parameter) => parameter.key.value === 'kind'),
        Option.flatMap((parameter) => Array.head(parameter.values)),
        Option.map((value) => ({ kind: value.value, at: value.position })),
      ),
  })

export const kindFaults = (kinds: DeclaredKinds, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  const known = [...boxKinds, ...kinds.box.map((declared) => declared.kind)]
  const refused = kinds.refusals
    .filter((refusal) => refusal.module === module.path)
    .flatMap((refusal) => faulty(TropeRefused({ reason: refusal.reason }), refusal.at).diagnostics)
  const unknown = openedBoxesOf(module.doc)
    .flatMap((opened) => Option.toArray(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.specifier)))
    .flatMap((specifier) => Option.toArray(writtenKind(specifier)))
    .filter(({ kind }) => !known.some((each) => sameKind(each, kind)))
    .flatMap(
      ({ kind, at }) => faulty(UnknownKind({ kind, kinds: known, on: 'box' }), at).diagnostics,
    )
  return [...refused, ...unknown, ...keysOutsideTrope(module), ...bodyFaults(kinds, module)]
}

const keysOutsideTrope = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  openedBoxesOf(module.doc)
    .filter((opened) => boxKind(opened.box) !== tropeKind)
    .flatMap((opened) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.specifier), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ParameterToken> => [],
        onSome: (specifier) => (specifier.type === 'Parameters' ? specifier.parameters : []),
      }),
    )
    .filter((parameter) => ['provides', 'anchors'].includes(parameter.key.value))
    .flatMap(
      (parameter) =>
        faulty(KeyOutsideTrope({ key: parameter.key.value }), parameter.key.position).diagnostics,
    )

## A body that fits its schema

A kind of box may give a schema for its body, as the chapter "The trope contract" describes. Loom reads the body of every box of such a kind and decodes it against the schema. It puts each fault on the field that is wrong, so the author corrects the body where it stands.

The data of a box is its body, the lines between the line that opens the box and the line that closes it. A line of YAML may look like a line of Loom, as a line of three dashes does, and a fence keeps such a line inert. So when a fence wraps the body, the data is the lines the fence holds, and `dataLines` gives them.

=>

const dataLines = (body: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  pipe(
    Option.all({ first: Array.head(body), last: Array.last(body) }),
    Option.filter(
      ({ first, last }) =>
        body.length > 1 &&
        Option.exists(openingFence(first.source), (fence) => closesFence(fence)(last.source)),
    ),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => body,
      onSome: () => pipe(body, Array.drop(1), Array.dropRight(1)),
    }),
  )

~

A fault lands on a place in the loom, and the reader of the data counts from the start of the data. A `Span` is a stretch of the data, from one offset to another. `wholeOf` gives the place of the data as a whole: its lines, or the line that opens the box when the box holds none. `pointIn` turns an offset in the data into a point of the loom, through the line that holds it.

=>

type Span = readonly [start: number, end: number]

const wholeOf = (opened: OpenedBox, lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): Position =>
  Option.match(Option.all({ first: Array.head(lines), last: Array.last(lines) }), {
    onNone: () => opened.opener.position,
    onSome: ({ first, last }) => ({ start: first.position.start, end: last.position.end }),
  })

const pointIn =
  (lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>, whole: Position) =>
  (offset: number): Point =>
    Option.match(
      Array.findLast(lines, (line) => line.position.start.offset <= whole.start.offset + offset),
      {
        onNone: () => whole.start,
        onSome: (line) =>
          shifted(line.position.start, whole.start.offset + offset - line.position.start.offset),
      },
    )

~

One reader reads both formats. A text of JSON is a text of YAML too, so the `yaml` package reads a body written in either, and it records where in the text each field stands. A `Path` leads to a field through the keys and the indexes above it. `spanAt` gives the span of the field a path leads to. A path may lead to a field the body does not hold, such as one the schema requires, and `spanAt` then gives the span of the nearest field above it that the body does hold.

=>

type Path = ReadonlyArray<PropertyKey>

const spanAt = (document: Document, path: Path): Option.Option<Span> =>
  pipe(
    Option.liftPredicate(document.getIn(path, true), isNode),
    Option.flatMap((node) => Option.fromNullishOr(node.range)),
    Option.map(([start, end]): Span => [start, end]),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Array.match(path, {
        onEmpty: () => Option.none(),
        onNonEmpty: (steps) => spanAt(document, Array.initNonEmpty(steps)),
      }),
    ),
  )

~

A body must first read as its format. `valueOf` gives the value a body holds, or the reason it does not read, for each format. A body of YAML reads when the reader finds no error in it, and the reader's first error is the reason. A body of JSON must be JSON itself, which is stricter than the YAML that reads it. JSON allows no comment and no key without quotes. So the contract's `parsedJson` function reads a body of JSON, and gives no reason of its own.

=>

const valueOf: Record<
  DataFormat,
  (text: string, document: Document) => Result.Result<unknown, Option.Option<string>>
> = {
  yaml: (_text, document) =>
    Option.match(Array.head(document.errors), {
      onNone: () => Result.succeed(document.toJS()),
      onSome: (error) => Result.fail(Option.some(error.message)),
    }),
  json: (text) => Result.mapError(parsedJson(text), () => Option.none()),
}

~

A value that reads is decoded by the kind's codec, and Effect answers with an issue for all that does not fit. An issue is a tree. A `Pointer` leads down a path, a `Composite` holds several issues, and a leaf says what is wrong where the tree ends. An `Encoding` wraps one issue, and an `AnyOf` holds the issues of a choice that none of its members fit. A `Filter` is a check a value failed, such as a least length, and it counts as a leaf. `misfitsIn` walks the tree and gives a `Misfit` for each leaf, the path of the field with the fault it earns. A key the schema requires and the body lacks earns a `MissingField`, and a key the schema does not allow earns an `UnknownField`. Any other leaf earns a `WrongField`, with the reason in the words `wordsOf` takes from Effect. `fieldOf` writes a path as a reader writes a field, such as `columns[1]` or `source.format`.

=>

type Misfit = { readonly path: Path; readonly fault: LoomFault }

const stepOf = (step: PropertyKey): string =>
  typeof step === 'number' ? `[${step}]` : `.${String(step)}`

const fieldOf = (path: Path): string => path.map(stepOf).join('').replace(/^\./, '')

const worded = SchemaIssue.makeFormatterStandardSchemaV1()

const wordsOf = (issue: SchemaIssue.Issue): string =>
  worded(issue)
    .issues.map((each) => each.message)
    .join(', ')

const misfitsIn: (
  kind: string,
  issue: SchemaIssue.Issue,
  path: Path,
) => ReadonlyArray<Misfit> = Match.fn(
  (_kind: string, issue: SchemaIssue.Issue, _path: Path) => issue,
).pipe(
  Match.tag('Pointer', (pointer, kind, _issue, path) =>
    misfitsIn(kind, pointer.issue, [...path, ...pointer.path]),
  ),
  Match.tag('Composite', (composite, kind, _issue, path) =>
    composite.issues.flatMap((each) => misfitsIn(kind, each, path)),
  ),
  Match.tag('Encoding', (encoding, kind, _issue, path) => misfitsIn(kind, encoding.issue, path)),
  Match.tag('AnyOf', (anyOf, kind, _issue, path) =>
    Array.match(anyOf.issues, {
      onEmpty: (): ReadonlyArray<Misfit> => [
        { path, fault: WrongField({ kind, field: fieldOf(path), reason: wordsOf(anyOf) }) },
      ],
      onNonEmpty: (issues) => issues.flatMap((each) => misfitsIn(kind, each, path)),
    }),
  ),
  Match.tag('MissingKey', (_matched, kind, _issue, path): ReadonlyArray<Misfit> => [
    { path, fault: MissingField({ kind, field: fieldOf(path) }) },
  ]),
  Match.tag('UnexpectedKey', (_matched, kind, _issue, path): ReadonlyArray<Misfit> => [
    { path, fault: UnknownField({ kind, field: fieldOf(path) }) },
  ]),
  Match.tag(
    'Filter',
    'InvalidType',
    'InvalidValue',
    'Forbidden',
    'OneOf',
    (unfit, kind, _issue, path): ReadonlyArray<Misfit> => [
      { path, fault: WrongField({ kind, field: fieldOf(path), reason: wordsOf(unfit) }) },
    ],
  ),
  Match.exhaustive,
)

~

`bodyFaultsOf` judges one box. It reads the box's data, and a body that does not read earns one `UnreadBody` fault, at the reader's first error, or over the whole of the data when the reader found none. A body that reads is decoded with every issue gathered, and with a key the schema does not allow counted against it. Each misfit lands on the span of its field.

`bodyFaults` judges every box of a loom whose kind holds data.

=>

const bodyFaultsOf = (kind: string, data: DataBody, opened: OpenedBox): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  const lines = dataLines(opened.body)
  const whole = wholeOf(opened, lines)
  const pointAt = pointIn(lines, whole)
  const text = lines.map((line) => line.source).join('')
  const document = parseDocument(text, { prettyErrors: false })
  const positionOf = (span: Option.Option<Span>): Position =>
    Option.match(span, {
      onNone: () => whole,
      onSome: ([start, end]) => ({ start: pointAt(start), end: pointAt(end) }),
    })
  return Result.match(valueOf[data.format](text, document), {
    onFailure: (reason) =>
      faulty(
        UnreadBody({ kind, format: data.format, reason: Option.getOrUndefined(reason) }),
        positionOf(Option.map(Array.head(document.errors), (error): Span => error.pos)),
      ).diagnostics,
    onSuccess: (value) =>
      Result.match(
        Schema.decodeUnknownResult(data.codec)(value, { errors: 'all', onExcessProperty: 'error' }),
        {
          onSuccess: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
          onFailure: (error) =>
            misfitsIn(kind, error.issue, []).flatMap(
              ({ path, fault }) => faulty(fault, positionOf(spanAt(document, path))).diagnostics,
            ),
        },
      ),
  })
}

const bodyFaults = (kinds: DeclaredKinds, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  openedBoxesOf(module.doc).flatMap((opened) =>
    pipe(
      Array.findFirst(kinds.box, (declared) => sameKind(declared.kind, boxKind(opened.box))),
      Option.flatMap((declared) =>
        Option.map(declared.data, (data) => bodyFaultsOf(declared.kind, data, opened)),
      ),
      Option.getOrElse((): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => []),
    ),
  )

## A key's card

A reader who rests on a key in braces wants to know two things: what the key takes, and who gave it to the bearer of the braces. The `keyCard` function writes both, as the card an editor shows over the key.

The card's first sentence reads the key's type, in the words the fault for a wrong value uses. So `status` takes `todo` or `done`, and `weight` takes a number. The function `heldIn` words one value, and `takingOf` words a key that takes one value or a list of them.

The second sentence names the giver. A trope gives a key to the bearers of a kind it declares, or to every heading, and Loom gives the core's keys to every bearer. The function `kindGiving` looks for the bearer's kind among the declared kinds of its sort, and keeps the kind when it lists the key. The table `sorts` names the sort of kind each bearer takes, since a heading takes a kind of section. The function `headingsGiving` looks for the key among those a trope gives every heading.

=>

const anyOf = new Intl.ListFormat('en', { type: 'disjunction' })

const heldIn: (type: ScalarType) => string = Match.type<ScalarType>().pipe(
  Match.withReturnType<string>(),
  Match.when('text', () => 'text'),
  Match.when('number', () => 'a number'),
  Match.when('boolean', () => '`true` or `false`'),
  Match.when('locale', () => 'a language tag, such as `et` or `pt-BR`'),
  Match.when('reference', () => 'the name of a section or a chapter'),
  Match.when({ choice: Match.any }, ({ choice }) =>
    anyOf.format(choice.map((word) => `\`${word}\``)),
  ),
  Match.exhaustive,
)

const takingOf = (key: string, type: ValueType): string =>
  isList(type)
    ? `\`${key}\` takes a list. Each value in the list is ${heldIn(type.list)}.`
    : `\`${key}\` takes ${heldIn(type)}.`

type Sort = {
  readonly noun: string
  readonly declared: (kinds: Declarations) => ReadonlyArray<{
    readonly kind: string
    readonly parameters: Keys
    readonly trope: string
  }>
}

const sorts: Record<BracesOn, Sort> = {
  heading: { noun: 'section', declared: (kinds) => kinds.section },
  anchor: { noun: 'anchor', declared: (kinds) => kinds.anchor },
  box: { noun: 'box', declared: (kinds) => kinds.box },
}

const kindGiving = (
  kinds: Declarations,
  on: BracesOn,
  kind: Option.Option<string>,
  key: string,
): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    kind,
    Option.flatMap((named) =>
      Array.findFirst(
        sorts[on].declared(kinds),
        (each) =>
          each.kind.toLowerCase() === named.toLowerCase() &&
          Option.isSome(keyType(each.parameters, key)),
      ),
    ),
    Option.map(
      (declared) =>
        `The trope \`${declared.trope}\` gives it to every ${sorts[on].noun} of the \`${declared.kind}\` kind.`,
    ),
  )

const headingsGiving = (kinds: Declarations, on: BracesOn, key: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.liftPredicate(kinds.headings, () => on === 'heading'),
    Option.flatMap(Array.findFirst((each) => each.key === key)),
    Option.map((declared) => `The trope \`${declared.trope}\` gives it to every heading.`),
  )

export const keyCard = (
  kinds: Declarations,
  on: BracesOn,
  kind: Option.Option<string>,
  key: string,
  type: ValueType,
): string =>
  [
    takingOf(key, type),
    pipe(
      kindGiving(kinds, on, kind, key),
      Option.orElse(() => headingsGiving(kinds, on, key)),
      Option.getOrElse(() => `Loom gives it to every ${on}.`),
    ),
  ].join('\n\n')

## What the registry draws on

From `effect` come the array, option, and result combinators, `Match` for the clashes and for the misfits of a box's data, `Order` for the order of the tropes, and `pipe` for the steps. From the Loom AST come the `Diagnostic` and `Position` a fault lands with, the section a manifest is, the `ParsedDocument` a chapter's frontmatter is read from, the `boxKinds`, `sectionKinds`, `anchorKinds`, and `loomKinds` the core keeps, the `identityWords` and `frontmatterKeys` a frontmatter already reads, the `headingKeys`, `boxKeys`, and `anchorKeys` it keeps with `keyType`, the `Keys` a kind's parameters are, the `ValueType` of one key, with `isList` and the `ScalarType` a key's card words, and the `Standing` of a kind of anchor, the `Zone` of a kind of loom, `boxKind` and `tropeKind`, which tell a trope box, the `FrontmatterTropeToken` a chapter names its trope with, and the tokens a box's braces arrive in, and from the corpus the `Catalogue` with `catalogueOf`, which builds one, the modules, and `openedBoxesOf`, which lists a loom's boxes. The product pass lends `isManifest` and `sectionNameOf`, which find a manifest's composed code, and the projection lends `fromCode`, which composes it, and `unfoundIn`, which names what the composition cannot find, over the `Code` the product model gives. The fault catalog lends `faulty` with the `TropeRefused`, `UnknownKind`, and `KeyOutsideTrope` faults, and `BracesOn`, which names a bearer of braces, and the contract lends `readManifest` with the shape it reads, the functions that read a manifest's tables and its keys for headings, `heldBy`, which reads what a kind of loom holds, and `parametersOf`, which reads a kind's parameters.

Judging a body draws on more. Effect's `Schema` decodes a body, and its `SchemaIssue` is the tree of what does not fit. The `yaml` package reads a body: `parseDocument` gives a `Document`, and `isNode` tells a field the document holds. The Loom AST gives the `Point` a span starts at, with `shifted`, which moves a point along its line, the `LoomWeft` a line of a body is, `openingFence` and `closesFence`, which tell a fence that wraps a body, the `DataFormat` a body is written in, and the `OpenedBox` a loom's box is. The fault catalog lends the `UnreadBody`, `MissingField`, `UnknownField`, and `WrongField` faults, with the `LoomFault` each is. The contract lends the `DataBody` of a kind, `dataOf`, which reads it, and `parsedJson`, which reads JSON.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Result, Schema, SchemaIssue } from 'effect'
import { type Document, isNode, parseDocument } from 'yaml'
import {
  type Diagnostic,
  type Point,
  type Position,
  shifted,
  byType,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { LoomSection, ParsedDocument } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import type { LoomWeft } from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'
import {
  anchorKeys,
  anchorKinds,
  boxKeys,
  boxKind,
  boxKinds,
  closesFence,
  type DataFormat,
  frontmatterKeys,
  type FrontmatterTropeToken,
  headingKeys,
  identityWords,
  isList,
  type Keys,
  keyType,
  loomKinds,
  openingFence,
  type ParameterToken,
  type ParametersToken,
  type ScalarType,
  sectionKinds,
  type SpecifierToken,
  type Standing,
  tropeKind,
  type ValueType,
  type Zone,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  type Catalogue,
  catalogueOf,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  type OpenedBox,
  openedBoxesOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { isManifest, sectionNameOf } from './ProductBuilder'
import type { Code } from '@athrio/loom-ast/ProductAst'
import { fromCode, unfoundIn } from './LoomVirtualCodeBuilder'
import {
  type BracesOn,
  faulty,
  KeyOutsideTrope,
  type LoomFault,
  MissingField,
  TropeRefused,
  UnknownField,
  UnknownKind,
  UnreadBody,
  WrongField,
} from './LoomFault'
import {
  anchorKindsOf,
  boxKindsOf,
  type DataBody,
  dataOf,
  headingKeysOf,
  heldBy,
  loomKindsOf,
  parametersOf,
  parsedJson,
  readManifest,
  sectionKindsOf,
  type TropeManifest,
} from './TropeManifest'

## The module [src/ast/DeclaredKinds.ts]

=>

::[What the registry draws on]

::[The registry]

::[A trope's root and its manifest]

::[Refusing a clash]

::[Gathering the registry]

::[Judging a loom against the registry]

::[A body that fits its schema]

::[A key's card]

## Testing the registry

The cases compile a small book that holds a chapter with a box and, in some cases, the roots and members of tropes. A loom written inside this loom's code would be read as part of it, so a fixture is a list of its lines, each one quoted, and `lines` joins them into the text a file holds. `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

`layerOf` gives the compiler a book held in memory, and `diagnosed` gives the messages of the diagnostics one loom raises. `asked` runs any one question to the compiler over a book, and `spansOf` gives each place an answer names as its loom, its line, and the characters it covers. `chapterWith` writes a chapter that holds one box with the given braces. `rootLoom` writes a trope chapter, whose title names the trope, with the given lines as its manifest's code. `memberLoom` writes a member of a trope the same way, with the trope its `Rooted in:` line names. `declaring` writes a manifest for the given kinds. `faultsOf` keeps the faults that speak of kinds, tropes and manifests, and `refusedFor` writes the message of a refusal.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import { defaultAnchorDelims } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import { DocumentSource, LoomCompiler } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { WorkspaceSettings } from '../src/WorkspaceSettings'
import { LoomDiskCache } from '../src/LoomDiskCache'
import { FileSystemSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const layerOf = (files: Record<string, string>) =>
  Layer.provide(
    LoomCompiler.layer,
    Layer.mergeAll(
      FileSystemSource,
      LoomDiskCache.none,
      Layer.succeed(DocumentSource, {
        read: (path: string) => Effect.succeed(files[path] ?? ''),
        list: Option.some(() => Effect.succeed(Object.keys(files))),
        hold: () => Effect.void,
        forget: () => Effect.void,
      }),
      Layer.succeed(WorkspaceSettings, {
        manifest: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none()),
        resolve: () =>
          Effect.succeed({
            delims: defaultAnchorDelims,
            primaryLanguage: 'typescript',
            variables: {},
            width: 100,
          }),
      }),
    ),
  )

const diagnosed = (files: Record<string, string>, entry: string) =>
  Effect.runPromise(
    LoomCompiler.pipe(
      Effect.flatMap((compiler) => compiler.diagnose(entry)),
      Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
    ),
  ).then((found) => found.map((d) => d.message))

const asked = <A>(
  files: Record<string, string>,
  use: (compiler: LoomCompiler['Service']) => Effect.Effect<A>,
) => Effect.runPromise(LoomCompiler.pipe(Effect.flatMap(use), Effect.provide(layerOf(files))))

const spansOf = (
  found: ReadonlyArray<{
    readonly path: string
    readonly range: {
      readonly start: { readonly line: number; readonly character: number }
      readonly end: { readonly character: number }
    }
  }>,
) =>
  found
    .map((place) => [
      place.path,
      place.range.start.line,
      place.range.start.character,
      place.range.end.character,
    ])
    .sort()

const cells = '/corpus/cells.loom'

const chapterWith = (braces: string) =>
  lines(
    '---',
    'Part I, Chapter 1: Cells',
    '---',
    '',
    '# Cells',
    '',
    `::: Cells ${braces}`,
    'flowchart LR',
    ':::',
  )

const withManifest = (
  identity: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  title: string,
  manifest: ReadonlyArray<string>,
) =>
  lines(
    '---',
    ...identity,
    '---',
    '',
    `# ${title}`,
    '',
    '## What it declares {Manifest}',
    '',
    '=>',
    ...manifest,
  )

const rootLoom = (title: string, manifest: ReadonlyArray<string>, part = '') =>
  withManifest([`${part}Trope Chapter: ${title}`], title, manifest)

const memberLoom = (title: string, trope: string, manifest: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
  withManifest([`Chapter: ${title}`, `Rooted in: ${trope}`], title, manifest)

const declaring = (...kinds: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  JSON.stringify({
    contract: '1',
    contributes: { kinds: { box: kinds.map((kind) => ({ kind, body: 'text' })) } },
  })

const faultsOf = (found: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
  found.filter((message) =>
    ['A box takes no', 'Loom ignores this trope', 'Only a trope chapter can hold a manifest'].some(
      (start) => message.startsWith(start),
    ),
  )

const refusedFor = (reason: string) => `Loom ignores this trope: ${reason}.`

## A kind nobody declares

A box of a kind that neither the core nor a trope gives is a fault, whether its braces name the kind with a label or with a `kind` parameter. The fault lists the core's five kinds.

=>

describe('a box of a kind nobody declares', () => {
  const unknown =
    'A box takes no `Mermaid` kind. It takes `Note`, `Chapter`, `Discussion`, `Trope`, and `Conclusion`.'

  it('is a fault, named by a label', async () => {
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}') }, cells))).toEqual([
      unknown,
    ])
  })

  it('is a fault, named by a parameter', async () => {
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [cells]: chapterWith('{kind=Mermaid}') }, cells))).toEqual([
      unknown,
    ])
  })
})

## A kind a trope declares

Once a trope's root declares a kind, a box of that kind raises nothing, in any case of letters, and in any loom of the book. A box of a kind still unknown then lists the declared kinds after the core's. A manifest's code composes like any section's, so an anchor in a manifest composes another section's code into it. That section may stand in the root or in a member of the same trope. Loom refuses a manifest whose anchor names a section in a chapter of another trope, or of no trope, and the refusal gives the name.

=>

describe('a box of a kind a trope declares', () => {
  const mermaid = '/corpus/mermaid/declaring.loom'
  const withTrope = (braces: string) => ({
    [cells]: chapterWith(braces),
    [mermaid]: rootLoom('Mermaid', [declaring('Mermaid')]),
  })

  it('raises nothing', async () => {
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(withTrope('{Mermaid}'), cells))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(withTrope('{kind=mermaid}'), cells))).toEqual([])
  })

  it('joins the kinds a fault lists', async () => {
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(withTrope('{Chart}'), cells))).toEqual([
      'A box takes no `Chart` kind. It takes `Note`, `Chapter`, `Discussion`, `Trope`, `Conclusion`, and `Mermaid`.',
    ])
  })

  it('may come from a manifest whose anchor composes another section', async () => {
    const withinRoot = lines(
      '---',
      'Trope Chapter: Mermaid',
      '---',
      '',
      '# Mermaid',
      '',
      '## The Mermaid kind {json}',
      '',
      '=>',
      '{ "kind": "Mermaid", "body": "mermaid" }',
      '',
      '## What it declares {Manifest}',
      '',
      '=>',
      '{ "contract": "1", "contributes": { "kinds": { "box": [',
      anchor('The Mermaid kind'),
      '] } } }',
    )
    const files = { [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}'), [mermaid]: withinRoot }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, cells))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, mermaid))).toEqual([])
  })

  const kindLoom = (identity: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    lines(
      '---',
      'Chapter: The Mermaid kind',
      ...identity,
      'Language: JSON',
      '---',
      '',
      '# The Mermaid kind',
      '',
      '=>',
      '{ "kind": "Mermaid", "body": "mermaid" }',
    )

  const anchoring = rootLoom('Mermaid', [
    '{ "contract": "1", "contributes": { "kinds": { "box": [',
    anchor('The Mermaid kind'),
    '] } } }',
  ])

  const kindAt = '/corpus/mermaid/the-kind.loom'

  it('may come from a manifest whose anchor composes a section of a member', async () => {
    const files = {
      [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}'),
      [mermaid]: anchoring,
      [kindAt]: kindLoom(['Rooted in: Mermaid']),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, cells))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, mermaid))).toEqual([])
  })

  const outside = refusedFor(
    'an anchor in its manifest names `The Mermaid kind`, and no chapter of the trope holds a section called that',
  )

  it('comes from no chapter outside a trope', async () => {
    const files = { [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}'), [mermaid]: anchoring, [kindAt]: kindLoom([]) }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, mermaid))).toEqual([outside])
  })

  it('comes from no chapter of another trope', async () => {
    const files = {
      [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}'),
      [mermaid]: anchoring,
      [kindAt]: kindLoom(['Rooted in: Charts']),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, mermaid))).toEqual([outside])
  })
})

## A root Loom refuses

A root that holds no manifest declares nothing and raises nothing, as a feature trope's root does. A root is refused when Loom cannot read it whole: when it holds two manifests, or a manifest that does not read. A refused root carries its fault in its own loom and declares none of its kinds, so a box of its kind stays a fault.

=>

describe('a root Loom refuses', () => {
  const root = '/corpus/mermaid/declaring.loom'
  const refused = async (text: string) => {
    const files = { [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}'), [root]: text }
    return {
      own: faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, root)),
      box: faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, cells)),
    }
  }

  it('declares nothing when it holds no manifest', async () => {
    const found = await refused(lines('---', 'Trope Chapter: Drawing', '---', '', '# Drawing'))
    expect(found.own).toEqual([])
    expect(found.box).toHaveLength(1)
  })

  it('holds two manifests', async () => {
    const twice = rootLoom('Mermaid', [
      declaring('Mermaid'),
      '',
      '## Again {Manifest}',
      '',
      '=>',
      declaring('Run'),
    ])
    expect((await refused(twice)).own).toEqual([refusedFor('it holds more than one manifest')])
  })

  it('holds a manifest that does not read', async () => {
    const found = await refused(rootLoom('Mermaid', ['{ "contract": "2" }']))
    expect(found.own).toEqual([refusedFor('it needs contract 2, and this Loom reads contract 1')])
    expect(found.box).toHaveLength(1)
  })
})

## One trope, one root, one meaning for a kind

A trope keeps one contract, so it has one root, and its manifest belongs there. When trope chapters of two parts share a title, the one whose loom comes first in the order of paths roots the trope, and the other is refused. A manifest on a member is faulted, and declares none of its kinds. A root that declares one of the core's own kinds is refused, and so is a root that declares a kind another trope declared first, or one kind twice.

=>

describe('roots and kinds that clash', () => {
  const first = '/corpus/tropes/a-charts.loom'
  const second = '/corpus/tropes/b-diagrams.loom'

  it('refuses a second root of one trope', async () => {
    const files = {
      [first]: rootLoom('Pictures', [declaring('Chart')], 'Part I, '),
      [second]: rootLoom('Pictures', [declaring('Diagram')], 'Part II, '),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, second))).toEqual([
      refusedFor('another trope chapter is called `Pictures`'),
    ])
  })

  it('reads nothing from a manifest off the root', async () => {
    const files = {
      [cells]: chapterWith('{Diagram}'),
      [first]: rootLoom('Pictures', [declaring('Chart')]),
      [second]: memberLoom('Diagrams', 'Pictures', [declaring('Diagram')]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, second))).toEqual([
      'Only a trope chapter can hold a manifest.',
    ])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, cells))).toHaveLength(1)
  })

  it('refuses a root that declares a core kind', async () => {
    const files = { [first]: rootLoom('Notes', [declaring('note')]) }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`note` is one of Loom's own kinds"),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses the later of two roots that declare one kind', async () => {
    const files = {
      [cells]: chapterWith('{Mermaid}'),
      [first]: rootLoom('Charts', [declaring('Mermaid')]),
      [second]: rootLoom('Diagrams', [declaring('Mermaid', 'Run')]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, second))).toEqual([
      refusedFor('the `Charts` trope declares `Mermaid` already'),
    ])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, cells))).toEqual([])
  })

  it('refuses a root that declares one kind twice', async () => {
    const files = { [first]: rootLoom('Charts', [declaring('Chart', 'chart')]) }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([
      refusedFor('it declares `chart` twice'),
    ])
  })
})

## A kind of section, taken or free

A kind of section clashes as a kind of box does. A root that declares one of the core's own kinds of section is refused, and so is the later of two roots that declare one kind. A root is refused as well when a kind of its declares a parameter under one of the core's own keys. A kind of box and a kind of section may share a name, since each table is judged alone. `sections` writes a manifest that declares the given kinds of section.

=>

describe('kinds of section that clash', () => {
  const first = '/corpus/tropes/a-decisions.loom'
  const second = '/corpus/tropes/b-choices.loom'
  const sections = (
    ...kinds: ReadonlyArray<{ kind: string; parameters?: Record<string, unknown> }>
  ): string => JSON.stringify({ contract: '1', contributes: { kinds: { section: kinds } } })

  it('refuses a root that declares a core kind of section', async () => {
    const files = { [first]: rootLoom('Decisions', [sections({ kind: 'tangle' })]) }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`tangle` is one of Loom's own kinds"),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses the later of two roots that declare one kind of section', async () => {
    const files = {
      [first]: rootLoom('Decisions', [sections({ kind: 'Option' })]),
      [second]: rootLoom('Choices', [sections({ kind: 'option' })]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, second))).toEqual([
      refusedFor('the `Decisions` trope declares `option` already'),
    ])
  })

  it("refuses a parameter under one of the core's own keys", async () => {
    const files = {
      [first]: rootLoom('Decisions', [sections({ kind: 'Option', parameters: { lang: 'text' } })]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, first))).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`lang` is one of Loom's own keys"),
    ])
  })

  it('lets a kind of box and a kind of section share a name', async () => {
    const both = JSON.stringify({
      contract: '1',
      contributes: { kinds: { box: [{ kind: 'Option', body: 'text' }], section: [{ kind: 'Option' }] } },
    })
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [first]: rootLoom('Decisions', [both]) }, first))).toEqual([])
  })
})

## A heading of a kind a trope declares

Once a trope's root declares a kind of section, a heading in any loom of the book may name it, with the parameters the kind takes. A parameter of the kind is read through its type, and it stays unknown on a heading of no kind. A kind nobody declares lists the declared kinds after the core's. The root itself may write a heading of the kind it declares, since the root is built with the registry like every loom, after its manifest is read with core Loom.

The registry is read before the looms, so a manifest that comes to declare another kind changes how every loom reads. The last case edits a manifest under one compiler and reads a chapter before and after.

`declaringSection` writes a manifest that declares one kind of section, whose headings take a `status`. `record` is a chapter with one heading that wears the given braces, and `book` sets it beside a root that declares the `Option` kind. `said` keeps the messages about what a heading takes.

=>

describe('a heading of a kind a trope declares', () => {
  const decisions = '/corpus/decisions/declaring.loom'
  const chapter = '/corpus/record.loom'

  const declaringSection = (kind: string): string =>
    JSON.stringify({
      contract: '1',
      contributes: {
        kinds: {
          section: [{ kind, parameters: { status: { choice: ['chosen', 'rejected'] } } }],
        },
      },
    })

  const record = (braces: string) =>
    lines(
      '---',
      'Part I, Chapter 1: A record',
      '---',
      '',
      '# A record',
      '',
      `## Postgres ${braces}`,
      '',
      'Prose.',
    )

  const book = (braces: string): Record<string, string> => ({
    [chapter]: record(braces),
    [decisions]: rootLoom('Decisions', [declaringSection('Option')]),
  })

  const said = (found: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    found.filter((message) => message.includes(' takes no '))

  it('raises nothing, named by a label or with a parameter the kind takes', async () => {
    expect(said(await diagnosed(book('{Option}'), chapter))).toEqual([])
    expect(said(await diagnosed(book('{kind=option status=chosen}'), chapter))).toEqual([])
  })

  it("reads the kind's parameter through its type", async () => {
    expect(said(await diagnosed(book('{kind=Option status=maybe}'), chapter))).toEqual([
      '`status` takes no `maybe`. It takes `chosen` and `rejected`.',
    ])
  })

  it("keeps the kind's parameter from a heading of no kind", async () => {
    expect(said(await diagnosed(book('{status=chosen}'), chapter))).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `status` key. It takes `lang` and `kind`.',
    ])
  })

  it('joins the kinds a fault lists', async () => {
    expect(said(await diagnosed(book('{kind=Bogus}'), chapter))).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `Bogus` kind. It takes `Tangle`, `Concept`, `Manifest`, and `Option`.',
    ])
  })

  it('is a fault while no trope declares it', async () => {
    expect(said(await diagnosed({ [chapter]: record('{kind=Option}') }, chapter))).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `Option` kind. It takes `Tangle`, `Concept`, and `Manifest`.',
    ])
  })

  it('may stand in the root that declares it', async () => {
    const root = lines(
      rootLoom('Decisions', [declaringSection('Option')]),
      '## Postgres {kind=Option status=chosen}',
      '',
      'Prose.',
    )
    const found = await diagnosed({ [decisions]: root }, decisions)
    expect(said(found)).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(found)).toEqual([])
  })

  it('reads every loom again when a manifest comes to declare another kind', async () => {
    const files = book('{kind=Choice}')
    const found = await Effect.runPromise(
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        const compiler = yield* LoomCompiler
        const before = yield* compiler.diagnose(chapter)
        files[decisions] = rootLoom('Decisions', [declaringSection('Choice')])
        yield* compiler.invalidate(decisions)
        const after = yield* compiler.diagnose(chapter)
        return {
          before: before.map((d) => d.message),
          after: after.map((d) => d.message),
        }
      }).pipe(Effect.provide(layerOf(files))),
    )
    expect(said(found.before)).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `Choice` kind. It takes `Tangle`, `Concept`, `Manifest`, and `Option`.',
    ])
    expect(said(found.after)).toEqual([])
  })
})

## Anchors, boxes, and headings a trope gives keys to

A trope may declare a kind of anchor, list the parameters of a kind of box, and let any heading carry a key. Each reaches every loom of the book, as a kind of section does. The cases compile a book whose trope declares all three, and a chapter that uses each.

`contributing` writes a manifest from what it contributes. `said` keeps the faults that speak of what a bearer takes, and `usingAll` is a chapter with a heading that carries a key for any heading, a box of the declared kind, and an anchor of the declared kind.

=>

describe('what a trope declares for anchors, boxes, and headings', () => {
  const notebooks = '/corpus/notebooks/declaring.loom'
  const second = '/corpus/notebooks/z-other.loom'
  const chapter = '/corpus/survey.loom'

  const contributing = (contributes: Record<string, unknown>): string =>
    JSON.stringify({ contract: '1', contributes })

  const all = contributing({
    kinds: {
      box: [{ kind: 'Dataset', body: 'yaml', parameters: { source: 'text' } }],
      anchor: [{ kind: 'Decision', parameters: { weight: 'number' } }],
    },
    headings: { parameters: { locale: 'locale' } },
  })

  const usingAll = (heading: string, box: string, braces: string) =>
    lines(
      '---',
      'Part I, Chapter 1: A survey',
      '---',
      '',
      '# A survey',
      '',
      `## The sample ${heading}`,
      '',
      `::: Sample ${box}`,
      'columns: [age]',
      ':::',
      '',
      '=>',
      'const sample = 1',
      '',
      '## The count [count.ts]',
      '',
      '=>',
      `${anchor('The sample')}${braces}`,
    )

  const said = (found: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    found.filter((message) => message.includes(' takes '))

  const book = (heading: string, box: string, braces: string) => ({
    [chapter]: usingAll(heading, box, braces),
    [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [all]),
  })

  it('lets every loom use them', async () => {
    const files = book('{locale=et}', '{kind=Dataset source="survey.csv"}', '{kind=Decision weight=3}')
    expect(said(await diagnosed(files, chapter))).toEqual([])
  })

  it('reads each of their values through its type', async () => {
    const files = book('{locale=pt_BR}', '{Dataset}', '{kind=Decision weight=heavy}')
    expect(said(await diagnosed(files, chapter))).toEqual([
      '`locale` takes a language tag, and `pt_BR` is not one. Write a tag such as `et` or `pt-BR`.',
      '`weight` takes a number, and `heavy` is not one. Write a number, such as `480`.',
    ])
  })

  it('lists a declared kind of anchor among the kinds an anchor takes', async () => {
    const files = book('', '{Dataset}', '{Verdict}')
    expect(said(await diagnosed(files, chapter))).toEqual([
      'An anchor takes no `Verdict` kind. It takes `To be settled`, `Module`, and `Decision`.',
    ])
  })

  it("refuses a root that declares one of the core's kinds of anchor", async () => {
    const files = {
      [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [contributing({ kinds: { anchor: [{ kind: 'module' }] } })]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, notebooks))).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`module` is one of Loom's own kinds"),
    ])
  })

  it("refuses a parameter of a box or an anchor under one of the core's own keys", async () => {
    const box = contributing({
      kinds: { box: [{ kind: 'Dataset', body: 'yaml', parameters: { anchors: 'reference' } }] },
    })
    const braces = contributing({
      kinds: { anchor: [{ kind: 'Decision', parameters: { provides: 'text' } }] },
    })
    expect(
      faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [box]) }, notebooks)),
    ).toEqual([refusedFor("`anchors` is one of Loom's own keys")])
    expect(
      faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [braces]) }, notebooks)),
    ).toEqual([refusedFor("`provides` is one of Loom's own keys")])
  })

  it("refuses a key for headings that is one of the core's own", async () => {
    const files = {
      [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [contributing({ headings: { parameters: { lang: 'text' } } })]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, notebooks))).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`lang` is one of Loom's own keys"),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses the later of two roots that give headings one key', async () => {
    const files = {
      [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [all]),
      [second]: rootLoom('Other', [contributing({ headings: { parameters: { locale: 'text' } } })]),
    }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, notebooks))).toEqual([])
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, second))).toEqual([
      refusedFor('the `Notebooks` trope lets any heading carry `locale` already'),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses a key for headings that a kind of section lists', async () => {
    const both = contributing({
      kinds: { section: [{ kind: 'Option', parameters: { status: 'text' } }] },
      headings: { parameters: { status: 'text' } },
    })
    const later = contributing({
      kinds: { section: [{ kind: 'Verdict', parameters: { locale: 'text' } }] },
    })
    expect(
      faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [both]) }, notebooks)),
    ).toEqual([refusedFor('`status` is a parameter of the `Option` kind of section already')])
    const files = { [notebooks]: rootLoom('Notebooks', [all]), [second]: rootLoom('Other', [later]) }
    expect(faultsOf(await diagnosed(files, second))).toEqual([
      refusedFor('the `Notebooks` trope lets any heading carry `locale` already'),
    ])
  })
})

## A parameter that names a section

A key declared as a reference holds the name of a section, and Loom treats the name as it treats an anchor's. It finds the section through the book, warns of a name that finds nothing, and rewrites the name when the section is renamed. The editor follows the name too: from each name of the value to what that name finds, and a rename or a search for references may start on the value as on an anchor. A chapter named by its number is renumbered with its chapter. The cases compile a book whose trope lets any heading carry `follows` and `related`, and gives its `Option` kind a `replaces`. The `record` chapter carries them, and the `rules` chapter holds the section they name.

`naming` keeps the faults that speak of a name.

=>

describe('a parameter that names a section', () => {
  const decisions = '/corpus/decisions/declaring.loom'
  const rulesAt = '/corpus/rules.loom'
  const recordAt = '/corpus/record.loom'

  const declaring = JSON.stringify({
    contract: '1',
    contributes: {
      kinds: { section: [{ kind: 'Option', parameters: { replaces: 'reference' } }] },
      headings: { parameters: { follows: 'reference', related: { list: 'reference' } } },
    },
  })

  const rules = lines(
    '---',
    'Part I, Chapter 2: Rules',
    '---',
    '',
    '# Rules',
    '',
    '## Overdrafts',
    '',
    'An overdraft is a balance below zero.',
  )

  const record = (braces: string) =>
    lines(
      '---',
      'Part I, Chapter 1: A record',
      '---',
      '',
      '# A record',
      '',
      `## Refusing a charge ${braces}`,
      '',
      'Prose.',
    )

  const book = (braces: string): Record<string, string> => ({
    [recordAt]: record(braces),
    [rulesAt]: rules,
    [decisions]: rootLoom('Decisions', [declaring]),
  })

  const naming = (found: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    found.filter((message) => message.includes(' names '))

  const unfound = (key: string, name: string) =>
    `\`${key}\` names \`${name}\`, and no section or chapter is called that. Name one that exists.`

  it('raises nothing for a name that finds a section or a chapter', async () => {
    expect(naming(await diagnosed(book('{follows="Rules, Overdrafts"}'), recordAt))).toEqual([])
    expect(naming(await diagnosed(book('{follows=Rules}'), recordAt))).toEqual([])
  })

  it('warns of a name that finds nothing, under the key that holds it', async () => {
    expect(naming(await diagnosed(book('{follows="Rules, Overdraughts"}'), recordAt))).toEqual([
      unfound('follows', 'Rules, Overdraughts'),
    ])
    expect(naming(await diagnosed(book('{kind=Option replaces=Nowhere}'), recordAt))).toEqual([
      unfound('replaces', 'Nowhere'),
    ])
  })

  it('judges each name of a list', async () => {
    const files = book('{related=["Rules, Overdrafts", Nowhere]}')
    expect(naming(await diagnosed(files, recordAt))).toEqual([unfound('related', 'Nowhere')])
  })

  it('is rewritten when its section is renamed, at the step that names the section', async () => {
    const braces = '{follows="Rules, Overdrafts"}'
    const heading = `## Refusing a charge ${braces}`
    const start = heading.indexOf('Overdrafts')
    const at = rules.indexOf('## Overdrafts') + 4
    const found = await asked(book(braces), (compiler) => compiler.rename(rulesAt, at))
    expect(spansOf(found)).toEqual(
      [
        [recordAt, 6, start, start + 'Overdrafts'.length],
        [rulesAt, 6, 3, 3 + 'Overdrafts'.length],
      ].sort(),
    )
  })

  it('is listed among the references to its section', async () => {
    const at = rules.indexOf('## Overdrafts') + 4
    const found = await asked(book('{follows="Rules, Overdrafts"}'), (compiler) =>
      compiler.references(rulesAt, at),
    )
    expect(spansOf(found).map(([path]) => path)).toEqual([recordAt, rulesAt])
  })

  const pathed = '{follows="Rules, Overdrafts"}'
  const within = (word: string): number => record(pathed).indexOf(word) + 1
  const inHeading = (word: string): number => `## Refusing a charge ${pathed}`.indexOf(word)

  it('is followed from each of its names to what the name finds', async () => {
    const section = await asked(book(pathed), (compiler) =>
      compiler.definition(recordAt, within('Overdrafts')),
    )
    const chapter = await asked(book(pathed), (compiler) =>
      compiler.definition(recordAt, within('Rules,')),
    )
    expect(spansOf(section === undefined ? [] : [section])).toEqual([
      [rulesAt, 6, 3, 3 + 'Overdrafts'.length],
    ])
    expect(spansOf(chapter === undefined ? [] : [chapter])).toEqual([
      [rulesAt, 1, 'Part I, Chapter 2: '.length, 'Part I, Chapter 2: Rules'.length],
    ])
  })

  it('starts a rename of the section it names, over its own words', async () => {
    const edits = await asked(book(pathed), (compiler) =>
      compiler.rename(recordAt, within('Overdrafts')),
    )
    const box = await asked(book(pathed), (compiler) =>
      compiler.renameRange(recordAt, within('Overdrafts')),
    )
    expect(spansOf(edits)).toEqual(
      [
        [recordAt, 6, inHeading('Overdrafts'), inHeading('Overdrafts') + 'Overdrafts'.length],
        [rulesAt, 6, 3, 3 + 'Overdrafts'.length],
      ].sort(),
    )
    expect(spansOf(box === undefined ? [] : [box])).toEqual([
      [recordAt, 6, inHeading('Rules'), inHeading('Rules') + 'Rules, Overdrafts'.length],
    ])
  })

  it('lists its section and every reference to it from the value', async () => {
    const found = await asked(book(pathed), (compiler) =>
      compiler.references(recordAt, within('Overdrafts')),
    )
    expect(spansOf(found).map(([path]) => path)).toEqual([recordAt, rulesAt])
  })

  it('colours its key as a keyword and its value as a name', async () => {
    const tokens = await asked(book(pathed), (compiler) => compiler.semanticTokens(recordAt))
    const onHeading = tokens
      .filter((token) => token.range.start.line === 6)
      .map((token) => [token.type, token.range.start.character, token.range.end.character])
    expect(onHeading).toEqual([
      ['namespace', 3, 3 + 'Refusing a charge'.length],
      ['keyword', inHeading('follows'), inHeading('follows') + 'follows'.length],
      ['namespace', inHeading('Rules'), inHeading('Rules') + 'Rules, Overdrafts'.length],
    ])
  })

  it('is rewritten when the chapter it names by number is renumbered', async () => {
    const numbered = '{follows="Part I, Chapter 2"}'
    const start = `## Refusing a charge ${numbered}`.indexOf('Chapter 2')
    const found = await asked(book(numbered), (compiler) =>
      compiler.rename(rulesAt, rules.indexOf('Chapter 2') + 1),
    )
    expect(spansOf(found)).toEqual(
      [
        [recordAt, 6, start, start + 'Chapter 2'.length],
        [rulesAt, 1, 'Part I, '.length, 'Part I, Chapter 2'.length],
      ].sort(),
    )
  })
})

## The rules a kind follows

A kind of section may hold no code, and a kind of anchor may keep to prose or to code. The editor offers a declared kind of anchor as a label only where the kind stands, and the case reads the kinds among the offers, leaving the keys out. The cases compile a book whose trope declares an `Option` kind of section with no code, and a `Decision` kind of anchor that stands in prose. A chapter then writes an option that holds code, composes an option into a file, and cites a decision from code and from prose.

=>

describe('the rules a kind follows', () => {
  const decisions = '/corpus/decisions/declaring.loom'
  const chapter = '/corpus/record.loom'

  const declaring = JSON.stringify({
    contract: '1',
    contributes: {
      kinds: {
        section: [{ kind: 'Option', code: 'none' }],
        anchor: [{ kind: 'Decision', standsIn: 'prose' }],
      },
    },
  })

  const book = (...body: ReadonlyArray<string>): Record<string, string> => ({
    [chapter]: lines('---', 'Part I, Chapter 1: A record', '---', '', '# A record', '', ...body),
    [decisions]: rootLoom('Decisions', [declaring]),
  })

  const ruled = (found: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    found.filter((message) => message.includes('` kind'))

  it('faults an arrow in a section of a kind that holds no code', async () => {
    const files = book('## Postgres {Option}', '', 'We chose it.', '', '=>', 'const chosen = 1')
    expect(ruled(await diagnosed(files, chapter))).toEqual([
      'A section of the `Option` kind holds no code. Quote this code in a fence, or move it to another section.',
    ])
  })

  it('raises nothing for such a section that holds words alone', async () => {
    const files = book('## Postgres {Option}', '', 'We chose it.')
    expect(ruled(await diagnosed(files, chapter))).toEqual([])
  })

  it('faults an anchor that composes such a section', async () => {
    const files = book(
      '## Postgres {Option}',
      '',
      'We chose it.',
      '',
      '## The store [store.ts]',
      '',
      '=>',
      anchor('Postgres'),
    )
    expect(ruled(await diagnosed(files, chapter))).toEqual([
      '`Postgres` is a section of the `Option` kind, which holds no code. Name the section whose code you mean.',
    ])
  })

  it('offers a declared kind as an anchor\'s label only where the kind stands', async () => {
    const offered = async (...body: ReadonlyArray<string>) => {
      const files = book('## Postgres', '', 'We chose it.', '', '## The store', '', ...body)
      const text = files[chapter] ?? ''
      const found = await asked(files, (compiler) =>
        compiler.completions(chapter, text.indexOf('{}') + 1),
      )
      return found
        .filter((completion) => !completion.text.endsWith('='))
        .map((completion) => completion.label)
    }
    expect(await offered(`The store follows ${anchor('Postgres')}{}.`)).toEqual([
      'To be settled',
      'Module',
      'Decision',
    ])
    expect(await offered('=>', `${anchor('Postgres')}{}`)).toEqual(['To be settled', 'Module'])
  })

  it('faults an anchor of a prose kind written in code, and none written in prose', async () => {
    const misplaced =
      'An anchor of the `Decision` kind belongs in prose, and this one stands in code. Move it into prose.'
    const inCode = book(
      '## Postgres',
      '',
      'We chose it.',
      '',
      '## The store [store.ts]',
      '',
      '=>',
      `${anchor('Postgres')}{Decision}`,
    )
    const inProse = book(
      '## Postgres',
      '',
      'We chose it.',
      '',
      '## The store',
      '',
      `The store follows ${anchor('Postgres')}{Decision}.`,
    )
    expect(ruled(await diagnosed(inCode, chapter))).toEqual([misplaced])
    expect(ruled(await diagnosed(inProse, chapter))).toEqual([])
  })
})

## A kind of loom a trope declares

A trope may declare a kind of loom, a document of its own beside the chapter and the discussion. The cases compile a book whose trope for tasks declares a `Tasks` kind of loom that holds a `Task` kind of section. `tasksRoot` writes the trope's root for the given kinds of loom, and `refusal` gives the reason Loom refuses it, if it does.

A root is refused when its kind of loom could be mistaken for a line the frontmatter already reads, and when the kind holds a kind of section its manifest does not declare.

A book that installs the trope may write a loom of the kind. `inBook` compiles one loom beside the trope's root, `tasksLoom` writes a loom of the `Tasks` kind, and `plans` is an ordinary chapter. A `Task` section is at home in a `Tasks` loom and a fault in the chapter. The `Tasks` kind holds no code, so an arrow in such a loom is a fault, a sink on one of its headings is a fault, and an anchor that composes one of its sections from a chapter is a fault. A loom of a kind nobody declares is a fault too.

A kind of loom also places its looms. `releasing` is a book with one part, which holds two chapters, a discussion, a loom of the `Tasks` kind, which sits at the back, and a loom of the `Checklist` kind, which sits in the body. Each kind counts its looms apart, so `Tasks 1`, `Chapter 1`, and `Checklist 1` name three looms of one part. Renumbering a loom from its identity line rewrites every path that names it by that number. `woven` weaves the book, and its menu lists the checklist after its part's chapters and the task list in the back matter, after the discussion.

=>

describe('a kind of loom a trope declares', () => {
  const root = '/corpus/tasks/declaring.loom'

  const tasksRoot = (...loom: ReadonlyArray<unknown>) =>
    rootLoom('Tasks', [
      JSON.stringify({
        contract: '1',
        contributes: { kinds: { loom, section: [{ kind: 'Task' }] } },
      }),
    ])

  const refusal = async (...loom: ReadonlyArray<unknown>) =>
    faultsOf(await diagnosed({ [root]: tasksRoot(...loom) }, root))

  const release = '/corpus/tasks/release.loom'
  const plans = '/corpus/plans.loom'

  const tasksKind = { kind: 'Tasks', numbered: true, zone: 'back', code: 'none', holds: ['Task'] }

  const inBook = (path: string, text: string) =>
    diagnosed({ [root]: tasksRoot(tasksKind), [path]: text }, path)

  const tasksLoom = (identity: string, ...body: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    lines('---', identity, '---', '', '# The next release', '', ...body)

  const chapter = (...body: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    lines('---', 'Chapter 1: Plans', '---', '', '# Plans', '', ...body)

  it('reads a loom of the kind, with a section of the kind it holds', async () => {
    const text = tasksLoom('Part XI, Tasks 1: The next release', '## Publish {Task}', '', 'Push.')
    expect(await inBook(release, text)).toEqual([])
  })

  it('faults a section of that kind in a chapter', async () => {
    expect(await inBook(plans, chapter('## Publish {Task}', '', 'Push the tag.'))).toEqual([
      'A section of the `Task` kind belongs in a loom of the `Tasks` kind. Move the section there, or take the kind off its heading.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults an arrow in a loom of a kind that holds no code', async () => {
    const text = tasksLoom('Tasks 1: The next release', '## Publish', '', 'Push.', '', '=>', 'const x = 1')
    expect(await inBook(release, text)).toEqual([
      'A loom of the `Tasks` kind holds no code. Quote this code in a fence, or move it to a chapter.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults a sink in such a loom', async () => {
    const text = tasksLoom('Tasks 1: The next release', '## Publish [publish.sh]', '', 'Push.')
    expect(await inBook(release, text)).toEqual([
      'A loom of the `Tasks` kind writes no file, so it takes no sink or `{Tangle}`. Remove it, or move the section into a chapter.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults an anchor that composes a section of such a loom', async () => {
    const files = {
      [root]: tasksRoot(tasksKind),
      [release]: tasksLoom('Tasks 1: The next release', '## Publish {Task}', '', 'Push.'),
      [plans]: chapter('## Script [release.ts]', '', '=>', anchor('The next release, Publish')),
    }
    expect(await diagnosed(files, plans)).toEqual([
      '`The next release, Publish` stands in a loom of the `Tasks` kind, which holds no code. Name the section whose code you mean.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults a loom of a kind nobody declares', async () => {
    const text = tasksLoom('Taks 1: The next release', '## Publish', '', 'Push.')
    expect(await inBook(release, text)).toEqual([
      'A loom takes no `Taks` kind. It takes `Chapter`, `Discussion`, and `Tasks`.',
    ])
  })

  const book = '/corpus/book.loom'

  const releasing = (link: string): Record<string, string> => ({
    [book]: lines('# Releasing Loom', '', 'A book.'),
    [root]: tasksRoot(tasksKind, { kind: 'Checklist', numbered: true }),
    '/corpus/cutting.loom': lines(
      '---',
      'Part XI: Releasing',
      'Chapter 1: Cutting a release',
      '---',
      '',
      '# Cutting a release',
    ),
    '/corpus/why.loom': lines('---', 'Part XI, Discussion 1: Why we tag', '---', '', '# Why we tag'),
    '/corpus/before.loom': lines(
      '---',
      'Part XI, Checklist 1: Before a release',
      '---',
      '',
      '# Before a release',
    ),
    [release]: tasksLoom('Part XI, Tasks 1: The next release', '## Publish {Task}', '', 'Push.'),
    [plans]: lines('---', 'Part XI, Chapter 2: Plans', '---', '', '# Plans [plans.ts]', '', '=>', anchor(link)),
  })

  const woven = (files: Record<string, string>) =>
    Effect.runPromise(
      LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((compiler) => compiler.weave(book)),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
      ),
    )

  it('finds a loom by the number its kind gives it, apart from the chapters', async () => {
    expect(await diagnosed(releasing('Part XI, Tasks 1'), plans)).toEqual([
      '`Part XI, Tasks 1` stands in a loom of the `Tasks` kind, which holds no code. Name the section whose code you mean.',
    ])
    expect(await diagnosed(releasing('Part XI, Chapter 1'), plans)).toEqual([
      'Chapter `Part XI, Chapter 1` holds no code in its opening section, so this anchor composes nothing. Name the section that holds the code instead.',
    ])
    expect(await diagnosed(releasing('Part XI, Tasks 2'), plans)).toEqual([
      'No section or chapter is called `Part XI, Tasks 2`. Write one, or label this anchor `{To be settled}`.',
    ])
  })

  it('renumbers a loom from its identity line, with every path that names it by number', async () => {
    const files = releasing('Part XI, Tasks 1')
    const found = await asked(files, (compiler) =>
      compiler.rename(release, (files[release] ?? '').indexOf('Tasks 1') + 1),
    )
    const inAnchor = anchor('Part XI, Tasks 1').indexOf('Tasks 1')
    expect(spansOf(found)).toEqual(
      [
        [plans, 7, inAnchor, inAnchor + 'Tasks 1'.length],
        [release, 1, 'Part XI, '.length, 'Part XI, Tasks 1'.length],
      ].sort(),
    )
  })

  it('colours the kind and its number as it colours a chapter number', async () => {
    const tokens = await asked(releasing('Part XI, Tasks 1'), (compiler) =>
      compiler.semanticTokens(release),
    )
    const onIdentity = tokens
      .filter((token) => token.range.start.line === 1)
      .map((token) => [token.type, token.range.start.character, token.range.end.character])
    expect(onIdentity).toEqual([
      ['namespace', 0, 'Part XI'.length],
      ['namespace', 'Part XI, '.length, 'Part XI, Tasks 1'.length],
      ['namespace', 'Part XI, Tasks 1: '.length, 'Part XI, Tasks 1: The next release'.length],
    ])
  })

  it('lists a loom in the zone its kind sits in', async () => {
    const site = await woven(releasing('Part XI, Chapter 1'))
    expect(site.nav.map((part) => [part.name, part.chapters.map((entry) => entry.title)])).toEqual([
      ['Releasing', ['Cutting a release', 'Plans', 'Before a release']],
      ['Orphan chapters', ['Tasks']],
      ['Back matter', ['Why we tag', 'The next release']],
    ])
  })

  it('reads a kind of loom that holds a kind of section its manifest declares', async () => {
    expect(await refusal({ kind: 'Tasks', zone: 'back', holds: ['Task'] })).toEqual([])
  })

  it('refuses a kind of loom the core keeps, in any case of letters', async () => {
    expect(await refusal({ kind: 'discussion' })).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`discussion` is one of Loom's own kinds"),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses a kind that holds a word of an identity line', async () => {
    expect(await refusal({ kind: 'Bonus Chapter' })).toEqual([
      refusedFor("`Bonus Chapter` holds `Chapter`, a word of Loom's own identity lines"),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses a kind that is a key of the frontmatter', async () => {
    expect(await refusal({ kind: 'Package' })).toEqual([
      refusedFor('`Package` is a line of the frontmatter already'),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses a kind declared twice', async () => {
    expect(await refusal({ kind: 'Tasks' }, { kind: 'tasks' })).toEqual([
      refusedFor('it declares `tasks` twice'),
    ])
  })

  it('refuses a kind that holds a kind of section nobody declares', async () => {
    expect(await refusal({ kind: 'Tasks', holds: ['Task', 'Bug'] })).toEqual([
      refusedFor(
        'its `Tasks` kind of loom holds `Bug`, and its manifest declares no kind of section called that',
      ),
    ])
  })
})

## A dataset judged by its schema

The cases compile a book whose trope declares the `Dataset` kind of box with a schema. A dataset names its `source`, lists its `columns` as text, and may count its `rows`, and it holds no other field. `judged` gives each fault a body raises as its message and the text the fault covers.

A body that fits raises nothing. A field the schema requires, a field it does not allow, and a value it does not accept each raise a fault of their own. The fault for a missing field covers the fields the body does hold, and each of the others covers the value it speaks of. An item of a list is named by its index. A body with no line is judged too, and its fault covers the line that opens the box.

A body that does not read as its format raises one fault, where the reader stopped. A body of JSON must be JSON itself, and YAML in its place is a fault over the whole body. A fence around a body keeps its lines from Loom, and the data is the lines the fence holds. A schema may stand in a section of its own, which the manifest draws in with an anchor. A kind that gives no schema leaves its body unread.

=>

describe('a dataset judged by its schema', () => {
  const notebooks = '/corpus/notebooks.loom'
  const notes = '/corpus/notes.loom'

  const schema = {
    type: 'object',
    required: ['source', 'columns'],
    additionalProperties: false,
    properties: {
      source: { type: 'string' },
      rows: { type: 'integer', minimum: 0 },
      columns: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    },
  }

  const datasets = (body: string, held: object = { schema }) =>
    rootLoom('Notebooks', [
      JSON.stringify({
        contract: '1',
        contributes: { kinds: { box: [{ kind: 'Dataset', body, ...held }] } },
      }),
    ])

  const notesWith = (rows: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
    lines(
      '---',
      'Part I, Chapter 1: Notes',
      '---',
      '',
      '# Notes',
      '',
      '::: Survey {Dataset}',
      ...rows,
      ':::',
    )

  const judged = async (root: string, rows: ReadonlyArray<string>) => {
    const text = notesWith(rows)
    const found = await asked({ [notebooks]: root, [notes]: text }, (compiler) =>
      compiler.diagnose(notes),
    )
    return found.map((fault) => [
      fault.message,
      text.slice(fault.position.start.offset, fault.position.end.offset),
    ])
  }

  it('raises nothing for a body that fits', async () => {
    expect(
      await judged(datasets('yaml'), ['source: data/survey.csv', 'rows: 1200', 'columns: [age]']),
    ).toEqual([])
  })

  it('faults a missing field, an unknown field, and a wrong value, each where it stands', async () => {
    expect(
      await judged(datasets('yaml'), ['source: data/survey.csv', 'rows: many', 'colums: [age]']),
    ).toEqual([
      ['A `Dataset` box takes no `colums` field. Remove it.', '[age]'],
      ['`rows` does not fit a `Dataset` box. Expected number.', 'many'],
      [
        'A `Dataset` box needs a `columns` field. Add it.',
        'source: data/survey.csv\nrows: many\ncolums: [age]\n',
      ],
    ])
  })

  it('names an item of a list by its index', async () => {
    expect(await judged(datasets('yaml'), ['source: a.csv', 'columns:', '  - age', '  - 3'])).toEqual([
      ['`columns[1]` does not fit a `Dataset` box. Expected string.', '3'],
    ])
  })

  it('judges a box with no body, on the line that opens it', async () => {
    expect(await judged(datasets('yaml'), [])).toEqual([
      ['This body does not fit a `Dataset` box. Expected object.', '::: Survey {Dataset}\n'],
    ])
  })

  it('faults a body that is not YAML, where the reader stopped', async () => {
    expect(await judged(datasets('yaml'), ['source: a.csv', 'source: b.csv'])).toEqual([
      ['A `Dataset` box holds YAML, and this body is not YAML: Map keys must be unique.', 's'],
    ])
  })

  it('reads a body of JSON, and puts a fault on its field', async () => {
    expect(
      await judged(datasets('json'), ['{ "source": "a.csv", "columns": ["age"] }']),
    ).toEqual([])
    expect(await judged(datasets('json'), ['{ "source": "a.csv", "columns": "age" }'])).toEqual([
      ['`columns` does not fit a `Dataset` box. Expected array.', '"age"'],
    ])
  })

  it('faults YAML where a kind holds JSON', async () => {
    expect(await judged(datasets('json'), ['source: a.csv', 'columns: [age]'])).toEqual([
      [
        'A `Dataset` box holds JSON, and this body is not JSON.',
        'source: a.csv\ncolumns: [age]\n',
      ],
    ])
  })

  it('reads the lines a fence holds', async () => {
    expect(
      await judged(datasets('yaml'), [
        '```yaml',
        'source: a.csv',
        '## the columns follow',
        'columns: [age, 3]',
        '```',
      ]),
    ).toEqual([['`columns[1]` does not fit a `Dataset` box. Expected string.', '3']])
  })

  it('reads a schema that its manifest draws in from a section of the trope', async () => {
    const cut = lines(
      '---',
      'Trope Chapter: Notebooks',
      '---',
      '',
      '# Notebooks',
      '',
      '## What a dataset holds {json}',
      '',
      '=>',
      JSON.stringify(schema),
      '',
      '## What it declares {Manifest}',
      '',
      '=>',
      '{ "contract": "1", "contributes": { "kinds": { "box": [',
      '{ "kind": "Dataset", "body": "yaml", "schema":',
      anchor('What a dataset holds'),
      '}] } } }',
    )
    expect(await judged(cut, ['source: a.csv', 'columns: age'])).toEqual([
      ['`columns` does not fit a `Dataset` box. Expected array.', 'age'],
    ])
  })

  it('leaves the body of a kind without a schema unread', async () => {
    expect(await judged(datasets('yaml', {}), ['source: [a.csv', 'rows: many'])).toEqual([])
  })
})

## What the editor offers in braces

The editor offers what braces may hold, and what a trope declares is part of the offer. The cases compile a book whose trope for tasks declares a `Tasks` kind of loom, which holds a `Task` kind of section. A `Task` takes a `status`, one of two words, and a list of `owners`. The trope also gives every heading a `translates` key. The loom `release` is of the `Tasks` kind, and `plans` is an ordinary chapter. Each holds one heading, `## Publish`, with the braces a case gives it.

A case marks the cursor with a bar in the heading's braces. The function `offered` takes the bar out, asks the compiler for its offers at that place, and gives each offer's label with the text it writes.

In empty braces the editor offers the kinds of section first and the keys after them. The `Task` kind is offered in the `Tasks` loom and not in the chapter, since its kind of loom is its scope. Once the braces name the `Task` kind, the editor offers that kind's keys beside the others, without the ones the braces hold already. After the equals sign of `status` it offers the two words the key takes, though braces that end on an equals sign read as no parameters yet. Under `kind` it offers the kinds of section.

A hover on a key shows its card. The function `cardOf` asks for the card over a word of the heading. The card of `status` says what the key takes and that the trope gives it to every section of the `Task` kind. The card of `translates` names the trope that gives it to every heading, and the card of `lang` names Loom.

=>

describe('what the editor offers in braces', () => {
  const root = '/corpus/tasks/declaring.loom'
  const release = '/corpus/tasks/release.loom'
  const plans = '/corpus/plans.loom'

  const tasksRoot = rootLoom('Tasks', [
    JSON.stringify({
      contract: '1',
      contributes: {
        kinds: {
          loom: [{ kind: 'Tasks', numbered: true, zone: 'back', code: 'none', holds: ['Task'] }],
          section: [
            {
              kind: 'Task',
              parameters: { status: { choice: ['todo', 'done'] }, owners: { list: 'text' } },
            },
          ],
        },
        headings: { parameters: { translates: 'reference' } },
      },
    }),
  ])

  const loomOf: Record<string, (braces: string) => string> = {
    [release]: (braces) =>
      lines(
        '---',
        'Tasks 1: The next release',
        '---',
        '',
        '# The next release',
        '',
        `## Publish ${braces}`,
      ),
    [plans]: (braces) =>
      lines('---', 'Chapter 1: Plans', '---', '', '# Plans', '', `## Publish ${braces}`),
  }

  const bookWith = (path: string, braces: string) => ({
    [root]: tasksRoot,
    [path]: loomOf[path]!(braces),
  })

  const offered = async (path: string, marked: string) => {
    const braces = marked.replace('|', '')
    const book = bookWith(path, braces)
    const offset = book[path]!.indexOf(braces) + marked.indexOf('|')
    const found = await asked(book, (compiler) => compiler.completions(path, offset))
    return found.map((offer) => [offer.label, offer.text])
  }

  const cardOf = async (braces: string, word: string) => {
    const book = bookWith(release, braces)
    const offset = book[release]!.indexOf(word) + 1
    return (await asked(book, (compiler) => compiler.hover(release, offset)))?.markdown
  }

  const core = ['Tangle', 'Concept', 'Manifest'].map((kind) => [kind, kind])

  it('offers the kinds of section in scope, then the keys a heading takes', async () => {
    const keys = [
      ['lang', 'lang='],
      ['kind', 'kind='],
      ['translates', 'translates='],
    ]
    expect(await offered(release, '{|}')).toEqual([...core, ['Task', 'Task'], ...keys])
    expect(await offered(plans, '{|}')).toEqual([...core, ...keys])
  })

  it('offers the keys of the kind the braces name, less the ones they hold', async () => {
    expect(await offered(release, '{kind=Task |}')).toEqual([
      ['lang', 'lang='],
      ['translates', 'translates='],
      ['status', 'status='],
      ['owners', 'owners='],
    ])
    expect(await offered(release, '{kind=Task st| owners=[ann]}')).toEqual([
      ['lang', 'lang='],
      ['translates', 'translates='],
      ['status', 'status='],
    ])
  })

  it('offers the words a key takes, in braces that read as no parameters yet', async () => {
    expect(await offered(release, '{kind=Task status=|}')).toEqual([
      ['todo', 'todo'],
      ['done', 'done'],
    ])
    expect(await offered(release, '{kind=Task owners=|}')).toEqual([])
  })

  it('offers the kinds of section under the kind key', async () => {
    expect(await offered(release, '{kind=T|}')).toEqual([...core, ['Task', 'Task']])
    expect(await offered(plans, '{kind=|}')).toEqual(core)
  })

  it('shows what a key takes, and who gave it to the heading', async () => {
    const braces = '{kind=Task status=todo translates=Plans lang=sh}'
    expect(await cardOf(braces, 'status')).toBe(
      '`status` takes `todo` or `done`.\n\nThe trope `Tasks` gives it to every section of the `Task` kind.',
    )
    expect(await cardOf(braces, 'translates')).toBe(
      '`translates` takes the name of a section or a chapter.\n\nThe trope `Tasks` gives it to every heading.',
    )
    expect(await cardOf(braces, 'lang')).toBe(
      '`lang` takes text.\n\nLoom gives it to every heading.',
    )
    expect(await cardOf(braces, 'todo')).toBeUndefined()
  })
})

## The test file [test/DeclaredKinds.test.ts]

=>

::[Testing the registry]

::[A kind nobody declares]

::[A kind a trope declares]

::[A root Loom refuses]

::[One trope, one root, one meaning for a kind]

::[A kind of section, taken or free]

::[A heading of a kind a trope declares]

::[Anchors, boxes, and headings a trope gives keys to]

::[A parameter that names a section]

::[The rules a kind follows]

::[A kind of loom a trope declares]

::[A dataset judged by its schema]

::[What the editor offers in braces]
